Consider an adaptive-optics system that uses a single near-infrared camera to image a guide star and obtains a well-sampled point-spread function (PSF) at each frame. The PSF is modeled as

  I(x) = |F[ A(u) exp( i φ(u) ) ]|^2 * T(x) + n(x),

where A(u) is the known pupil amplitude, φ(u) = Σ_k a_k Z_k(u) is the residual wavefront phase expanded in Zernike (or analogous) modes Z_k with unknown coefficients a_k (including a_focus and other low-order terms), F denotes the Fraunhofer transform, T(x) accounts for detector sampling/integration, and n(x) is photon and read noise. The PSF is sampled finer than Nyquist for spot-centroid estimation, as required by focal-plane phase-retrieval techniques of the LIFT type.

In real observing conditions, the measured intensity I(x) is additionally affected by confounding image-elongating and image-distorting effects, each of which can mimic low-order aberrations:
 (i) wind-shake, modeled as a stochastic time-correlated tip-tilt jitter convolved into the long-exposure PSF;
 (ii) anisoplanatism for an off-axis guide star, producing an angle-dependent residual phase φ_aniso(u; θ) that elongates the PSF along the off-axis direction;
 (iii) laser-guide-star (LGS) aberrations arising from the finite thickness and structure of the sodium layer, producing a guide-star-specific quasi-static elongation pattern;
 (iv) calibration errors in the non-common-path aberration model and in the PSF forward model;
 (v) telescope phasing errors for a segmented primary, producing additional low-order-like signatures.

Assume one is allowed to inject a known dynamic probe aberration φ_probe(u, t) (rather than only a static diversity bias) into the deformable mirror.

Problem. Devise an estimator â_k(t) of the low-order Zernike coefficients — in particular the defocus coefficient a_focus — from the time series of finely sampled PSFs I(x,t) such that:
 (a) â_focus is asymptotically unbiased and consistent under realistic atmospheric and telescope conditions including the effects (i)–(v) above;
 (b) the estimator is provably identifiable, i.e., it separates the intentionally probed low-order signal from each of the confounding effects (i)–(v);
 (c) it does not require a second dedicated visible or near-infrared wavefront-sensing camera;
 (d) it operates at the fine PSF sampling required by focal-plane phase retrieval, not merely at the coarse sampling sufficient for spot-displacement estimation.

Provide such an estimator together with conditions on the probe φ_probe(u,t), the PSF sampling, and the temporal bandwidth under which identifiability and consistency can be guaranteed, or prove that no such estimator exists under stated minimal assumptions.

